# Libraries this AppImage must never bundle, on top of the vendored
# pkg2appimage excludelist.
#
# The vendored list assumes a self-contained application. This one is not:
# it deliberately ships no libVLC and instead dlopens the host's, which in
# turn dlopens ~400 host plugins into the same process.
#
# Those plugins resolve their own dependencies against whatever is already
# loaded. The bundled python3 has DT_RPATH (not RUNPATH) of $ORIGIN/../lib,
# and RPATH is inherited by every dlopen in the process and outranks
# LD_LIBRARY_PATH, so anything in the AppDir's usr/lib wins for the host's
# plugins too.
#
# glib is the case that bites. libqxcb.so needs libglib-2.0 and libgthread-2.0
# but not libgobject/libgio/libgmodule, so an ldd walk bundles a partial,
# older glib. The host's VLC plugins then link against that instead of the
# host's complete, newer one. Measured on Ubuntu 22.04 build host against a
# 26.04 target: 8 of 382 plugins failed with "undefined symbol: g_dir_unref",
# among them libavcodec_plugin.so and libavformat_plugin.so, so H.264, HEVC,
# AAC and MP3 would not decode.
#
# Bundling the complete glib family is not the fix; it fails in the mirror
# direction, with the host's newer plugins calling into an older ABI. The fix
# is that no glib-family library enters the AppDir at all. Every desktop that
# has VLC has glib, so relying on the host's is safe.
#
# The acceptance test is a dlopen sweep of every host VLC plugin under
# AppRun's environment. It must report zero failures.

libglib-2.0.so.0
libgthread-2.0.so.0
libgobject-2.0.so.0
libgio-2.0.so.0
libgmodule-2.0.so.0
